<CounterfeitStudy
  title="Four Truths and One Word"
  subtitle="Go Deeper: The Thirty-Third of the 38 Blessings"
  deck="Suffering is real. The question is where it came from."
  description="The thirty-third of the 38 Blessings, the Four Noble Truths, set beside Scripture: suffering is real, but it came from sin, and the cure is redemption."
>
    <h2>The Teaching</h2>
    <p>The thirty-third blessing is "Recognizing the Four Noble Truths," the heart of Buddhism. Tradition says the Buddha first taught them at a deer park near Benares after his awakening. They run in four steps. First, life is <em>dukkha</em>, suffering or lasting dissatisfaction. Second, suffering is caused by <em>taṇhā</em>, craving. Third, suffering ends when craving ends. Fourth, the way to end craving is the Eightfold Path: right view, right intention, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right concentration.</p>
    <h2>What the Word Says</h2>
    <p>Scripture agrees that suffering is real. "Man that is born of a woman is of few days, and full of trouble" (Job 14:1), and "the whole creation groaneth and travaileth in pain together" (Romans 8:22).</p>
    <p>Scripture disagrees about the cause. Suffering did not begin with desire. It began with sin. The ground was cursed, pain came into childbirth, and death followed after Adam broke Yahuah's command (Genesis 3:16-19). "By one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin" (Romans 5:12), and "sin is the transgression of the law" (1 John 3:4).</p>
    <p>Desire itself is not the enemy in Scripture. "Delight thyself also in Yahuah; and he shall give thee the desires of thine heart" (Psalm 37:4). The Tenth Commandment forbids coveting what belongs to a neighbor (Exodus 20:17). It does not forbid wanting.</p>
    <p>The cure is not the end of desire but redemption. Yahuah is the redeemer who paid the price, and the price was His Son (John 3:16; Isaiah 43:14). The way is not a program but a Person: "I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me" (John 14:6).</p>
    <h2>Where It Turned</h2>
    <p>Each of the four truths keeps a piece of the real thing and turns it. Suffering is kept, but sin is replaced by craving. The end of suffering is kept, but resurrection is replaced by extinction. The way is kept, but the Son who leads to the Father is replaced by eight steps a man walks alone. And truth itself is kept, but the truth Yahuah spoke is replaced by insight a man found sitting under a tree.</p>
    <p>That last detail is worth noticing without pressing it too hard. Buddhism's founding moment is an awakening under a tree, the Bodhi tree, that is said to have opened a man's eyes. Scripture's first fall also came at a tree "to be desired to make one wise," with the promise that "your eyes shall be opened" (Genesis 3:5-6).</p>
    <h2>The Verdict</h2>
    <p>Scripture has one Truth, and it is spoken, not discovered: "Thy word is truth" (John 17:17), and "thy law is the truth" (Psalm 119:142). Four truths found by a man cannot stand in its place.</p>
    <p class="cfs-ready"><strong>Ready Answer.</strong> Buddhism says suffering comes from desire; Scripture says it comes from sin, "the transgression of the law" (1 John 3:4), and the cure is not to stop wanting but to be redeemed.</p>
    <p>Revelation closes with the One on the throne saying, "Behold, I make all things new... these words are true and faithful" (Revelation 21:5). The truth that ends suffering is His word, not man's insight.</p>
